The '''2012–13 season''' was the 119th season of competitive football by [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]].

== Review ==
Celtic went about their 2012–13 league campaign in a steady if not always inspiring manner.<ref name="wsc1">{{cite web |url=http://www.wsc.co.uk/wsc-daily/1163-june-2013/9868-2012-13-season-in-review-celtic |title=When Saturday Comes - 2012-13 season in review: Celtic |publisher=Wsc.co.uk |date=9 July 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112052057/http://www.wsc.co.uk/wsc-daily/1163-june-2013/9868-2012-13-season-in-review-celtic |archive-date=12 November 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref> [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]], [[Inverness Caley Thistle]] and briefly [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] all either had spells at the top of the table, or thereabouts, but by Christmas Celtic had found a steady run of form to pull themselves comfortably clear of the pack.<ref name="wsc1" /> Celtic eventually clinched their 44th League title on 21 April 2013 with a 4–1 win over Inverness CT at [[Celtic Park|Parkhead]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://spfl.co.uk/news/article/celtic-clinch-title-in-style-2013-04-21/ |title=Celtic clinch title in style |publisher=Spfl.co.uk |date=21 April 2013 |access-date=12 November 2013 |archive-date=12 November 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112051049/http://spfl.co.uk/news/article/celtic-clinch-title-in-style-2013-04-21/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Celtic's run in the League Cup saw them comfortably dispose of [[Raith Rovers F.C.|Raith Rovers]] (4–1)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19632469 |title=BBC Sport - Celtic 4-1 Raith Rovers |publisher=Bbc.co.uk |date=25 September 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> and [[St Johnstone F.C.|St Johnstone]] (5–0)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20053650 |title=BBC Sport - Celtic 5-0 St Johnstone |publisher=Bbc.co.uk |date=30 October 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> in the earlier rounds. St Mirren, however, pulled off an unexpected but deserved 3–2 win over Celtic in the semi-final.<ref>{{cite web|last=Campbell |first=Andy |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21106906 |title=BBC Sport - St Mirren 3-2 Celtic |publisher=Bbc.co.uk |date=27 January 2013 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref>

Celtic's 2013 Scottish Cup campaign began inauspiciously with a home 1–1 draw against Second Division side, [[Arbroath F.C.|Arbroath]]. Celtic won the replay 1–0 with an [[Adam Matthews]] goal, but were again unconvincing. Celtic played Raith Rovers at [[Starks Park]] in the next round and eventually overcame a defensive performance by Raith to win 3–0, with [[Kris Commons]], [[Charlie Mulgrew]] and [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] scoring the goals.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21218450 |title=Raith Rovers 0–3 Celtic |first=Stevie |last=Miller |work=BBC Sport |publisher=BBC |date=3 February 2013 |access-date=20 April 2013}}</ref> In the quarter-finals, Celtic were drawn away to [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]], who had eliminated Celtic from the [[2012–13 Scottish League Cup]] a month beforehand.<ref name = "mirren"/> Early goals by [[Joe Ledley]] and [[Anthony Stokes]], either side of an equaliser by St Mirren, gave Celtic a 2–1 victory at [[St Mirren Park]].<ref name = "mirren">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21556779 |first=Kenny |last=Crawford |title=St Mirren 1–2 Celtic |work=BBC Sport |publisher=BBC |date=2 March 2013 |access-date=20 April 2013}}</ref> In the semi-final at Hampden Park, Celtic won a thrilling match by 4–3 after extra time against [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]].<ref name = "united">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22056531 |title=Dundee Utd 3–4 Celtic |first=Clive |last=Lindsay |work=BBC Sport |publisher=BBC |date=14 April 2013 |access-date=20 April 2013}}</ref> A Kris Commons goal gave Celtic an early lead, but United scored twice midway through the first half to lead 2–1.<ref name = "united"/> [[Victor Wanyama]] scored an equaliser almost immediately and Commons scored another goal after 60 minutes to give Celtic a 3–2 lead.<ref name = "united"/> [[Jon Daly (footballer)|Jon Daly]] scored his second goal of the game to equalise and force extra time.<ref name = "united"/> Soon after Daly had hit the post, Anthony Stokes scored the winning goal.<ref name = "united"/>

Celtic met Hibernian in the Scottish Cup Final on 26 May 2013. For the first time in its history, the Cup Final was played on a Sunday.<ref name = "sunday">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16685196|title=Sunday switch for 2013 Scottish Cup final|work=BBC Sport|publisher=BBC|date=23 January 2012|access-date=13 April 2013}}</ref> This was done to comply with [[UEFA]] regulations which prohibit televised matches being played on the same day as the [[UEFA Champions League Final]]. Celtic won the cup and clinched a League and Cup double with a 3–0 win; two first-half goals from [[Gary Hooper]] putting Celtic into a comfortable position before Joe Ledley added a third late on in the second half to put the matter beyond doubt.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22585339 |title=BBC Sport - Scottish Cup final: Hibernian 0-3 Celtic |publisher=Bbc.co.uk |date=26 May 2013 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref>

As Scottish Champions from the previous season, Celtic had the opportunity to take part in the 2012-13 UEFA Champions League. However, two qualifying rounds had first to be negotiated, which Celtic managed to do with 4–1 and 4–0 aggregate wins over [[Helsingin Jalkapalloklubi|HJK Helsinki]] and [[Helsingborgs IF]]. Celtic, as fourth-ranked seeds, found themselves drawn in [[2012–13 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group G|Group G]] with [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] and [[FC Spartak Moscow|Spartak Moscow]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19424943 |title=BBC Sport - Champions League: Celtic to face Barcelona, Benfica & Spartak |publisher=Bbc.co.uk |date=30 August 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref>

On 2 October 2012, Celtic achieved their first ever away win in the group stages of the Champions League with a 3–2 win in Russia over Spartak Moscow, [[Georgios Samaras]] scoring the winning goal in the 90th minute.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Campbell |first1=Andy |title=Spartak Moscow 2-3 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/19709684 |access-date=29 September 2020 |work=BBC Sport |date=2 October 2012}}</ref> Celtic's home match with Barcelona in November 2012 coincided with the week of Celtic's 125th Anniversary.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Colett |first1=Mike |title=Celtic's win over Barca gives fans reason to believe |url=https://de.reuters.com/article/soccer-champions-celtic-barcelona-idINDEE8A708K20121108 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201126133551/https://de.reuters.com/article/soccer-champions-celtic-barcelona-idINDEE8A708K20121108 |url-status=dead |archive-date=26 November 2020 |access-date=29 September 2020 |work=Reuters |date=8 November 2012}}</ref> As such, an '[[Ultras]]' styled section of the Celtic support called the [[Green Brigade]] organised a full stadium pre-match card display (a '[[tifo]]') to celebrate the club's 125th anniversary.<ref>{{cite news|title=Incredible pre-match display from supporters |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3319 |work=celticfc.net |publisher=Celtic F.C. |date=7 November 2012 |access-date=8 November 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112134748/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3319 |archive-date=12 November 2013 }}</ref> The display featured a [[Celtic cross]], green and white hoops and ''125 Celtic'' in written form, with supporters earning the praise of club chairman [[Peter Lawwell]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Peter Lawwell congratulates team and support on collective effort |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3325 |work=celticfc.net |publisher=Celtic F.C. |date=8 November 2012 |access-date=8 November 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112134645/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=3325 |archive-date=12 November 2013 }}</ref> A memorable night was completed when goals from Victor Wanyama and 18-year-old striker [[Tony Watt]] gave Celtic a shock 2–1 win over Barcelona.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20150451 |title=BBC Sport - Celtic 2-1 Barcelona |publisher=Bbc.co.uk |date=7 November 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> Goalkeeper [[Fraser Forster]] produced an outstanding performance in the game, winning the praise of the Spanish media who nicknamed him "''La Gran Muralla''" ("The Great Wall").<ref>{{cite news| url = http://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/2012/11/08/fraser-forster-great-wall_n_2091816.html | title = Fraser Forster Great Wall | work = HuffPost| date = 8 November 2012 | access-date = 8 November 2012 }}</ref>

Celtic secured their progress to the knockout stages of the Champions League on 5 December 2012 with a 2–1 home win over Spartak Moscow, Kris Commons scoring the winning goal in 82 minutes with a penalty.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20522174 |title=BBC Sport - Celtic 2-1 Spartak Moscow |publisher=Bbc.co.uk |date=5 December 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> Celtic were drawn against [[Juventus FC|Juventus]], where they succumbed to a 5–0 aggregate defeat in the spring 2013 to go out of the tournament.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crawford |first1=Kenny |title=Juventus 2-0 Celtic (agg 5-0) |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/21598002 |access-date=29 September 2020 |work=BBC Sport |date=6 March 2013}}</ref> Despite the result against Juventus, Celtic and Neil Lennon won praise for their Champions League campaign.<ref>{{cite web|first=Tony |last=Haggerty |url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-boss-neil-lennon-just-2670834 |title=Celtic boss Neil Lennon is just like my old man Harry in the dugout, claims ex-England star Jamie Redknapp |publisher=Daily Record |date=3 November 2013 |access-date=12 November 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131107032139/http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-boss-neil-lennon-just-2670834 |archive-date=7 November 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|author=Jeff Stelling |url=http://www.skysports.com/opinion/story/0,25212,12088_8235533,00.html |title=Celtic win over Barcelona compels Jeff Stelling to tackle a challenging problem |publisher=Sky Sports |date=8 November 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|author=Jeff Stelling |url=http://www.skysports.com/opinion/story/0,25212,12088_8313877,00.html |title=Man City could learn a lot from Celtic's Champions League exploits |publisher=Sky Sports |date=6 December 2012 |access-date=12 November 2013}}</ref>

